{
  "page": 5,
  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "4. Scenario-Based Reachability with Norm Balls",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        93.5718994140625,
        338.0704040527344,
        104.31961822509766
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The method presented here uses trajectory data to approximate the reachable set with a p-norm ball,",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        115.17363739013672,
        522.0003051757812,
        124.92637634277344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "that is a set of the form",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        128.7236328125,
        190.5928192138672,
        138.4763641357422
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ˆR[t0,t1](A, b) = {x : ||Ax −b||p ≤1}",
      "bbox": [
        222.7259979248047,
        139.340087890625,
        389.2735595703125,
        154.15562438964844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(6)",
      "bbox": [
        509.2799987792969,
        142.27264404296875,
        522.0,
        152.02537536621094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where x, b ∈Rn, A ∈Rn×n, and p may be either any real ≥1 or ∞. The class of p-norm balls",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        159.13247680664062,
        521.9985961914062,
        171.42539978027344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "encompasses several types of sets that are popular in reachability analysis. For instance, choosing",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        175.2216796875,
        522.0001220703125,
        184.9744110107422
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "p = 2 leads to the class of ellipsoids, and choosing p = ∞and restricting A to be diagonal leads to",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        187.9862060546875,
        521.999755859375,
        198.52439880371094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the class of multidimensional intervals.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        202.3206787109375,
        260.9346618652344,
        212.0734100341797
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "4.1. Unconstrained Norm Balls",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        229.93377685546875,
        236.0619354248047,
        239.74105834960938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We ﬁrst consider the general case, where A may be any symmetric matrix. We use −log det A",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        248.5882568359375,
        521.9998168945312,
        259.1264343261719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "as a proxy for the volume of ˆR[t0,t1](A, b). The value −log det(A) is directly proportional to the",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        259.99114990234375,
        521.996826171875,
        274.80572509765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "volume of a 2-norm ball; by the equivalence of norms, it is a suitable proxy for the volume of other",
      "bbox": [
        90.00003051757812,
        276.29718017578125,
        521.9964599609375,
        286.2244567871094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "p-norm balls as well. Using this proxy, the minimum-volume ϵ-accurate reachable set problem (2)",
      "bbox": [
        90.00003814697266,
        289.84619140625,
        522.004150390625,
        299.7734680175781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "becomes",
      "bbox": [
        90.00006103515625,
        303.57073974609375,
        128.1710205078125,
        313.3234558105469
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "arg min",
      "bbox": [
        202.7320556640625,
        314.1767272949219,
        236.16844177246094,
        323.929443359375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A,b",
      "bbox": [
        221.52206420898438,
        323.39483642578125,
        233.84384155273438,
        330.4722900390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "−log det A",
      "bbox": [
        248.55506896972656,
        313.3912658691406,
        300.52490234375,
        323.689453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "s.t.",
      "bbox": [
        202.73208618164062,
        337.6397399902344,
        215.4630126953125,
        347.3924560546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PZ(||AZ −b||p −1 ≤0) ≤1 −ϵ,",
      "bbox": [
        246.13108825683594,
        336.8542785644531,
        399.3089294433594,
        348.7884521484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(7)",
      "bbox": [
        509.28009033203125,
        325.9257507324219,
        522.0001220703125,
        335.678466796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where Z is a random variable whose support is R[t0,t1]. Since −log det A and ||AZ −b||p −1 are",
      "bbox": [
        90.00009155273438,
        355.94830322265625,
        522.0001220703125,
        368.6167907714844
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "convex in A and b, (7) is a convex chance-constrained optimization problem, meaning that it can be",
      "bbox": [
        90.00009155273438,
        370.10821533203125,
        521.9968872070312,
        380.0354919433594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "solved using scenario optimization.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0000991821289,
        383.831787109375,
        244.374755859375,
        393.5845031738281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We use the transition function Φ to construct a suitable Z. Speciﬁcally, let X0, U, and D be",
      "bbox": [
        106.93609619140625,
        397.20721435546875,
        522.0003051757812,
        407.9603271484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "given random variables whose supports are the initial set, input set, and disturbance set. Then the",
      "bbox": [
        90.0001220703125,
        410.9307861328125,
        522.0001831054688,
        420.6835021972656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "support of the random variable Z = Φ(t1; t0, X0, U, D) is exactly the reachable set.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0001220703125,
        424.30523681640625,
        455.6031799316406,
        435.058349609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The approach is outlined in Algorithm 1. The output is a matrix A and vector b for a norm",
      "bbox": [
        106.93609619140625,
        437.854248046875,
        522.0045166015625,
        447.7815246582031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ball reachable set estimate that is ϵ-accurate with high probability with respect to Z. This is the",
      "bbox": [
        90.00006103515625,
        451.40325927734375,
        521.995849609375,
        461.3305358886719
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "probabilistic guarantee of correctness, which we formally present with Theorem 2.",
      "bbox": [
        90.00003051757812,
        465.1278076171875,
        450.5238037109375,
        474.8805236816406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Theorem 2",
      "bbox": [
        90.00003051757812,
        486.47088623046875,
        140.40008544921875,
        496.2781677246094
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Let ϵ, δ ∈(0, 1), and let X0, U, and D be random variables over X0, U, and D",
      "bbox": [
        149.91702270507812,
        485.81634521484375,
        521.6929321289062,
        497.1813659667969
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "respectively, and let Z = Φ(t1; t0, X0, U, D). Denote by PS the probability measure corresponding",
      "bbox": [
        90.00003051757812,
        499.97723388671875,
        521.995361328125,
        511.34161376953125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to the multisample of N =",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        516.2462158203125,
        203.93125915527344,
        525.9335327148438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "l",
      "bbox": [
        206.95999145507812,
        511.27178955078125,
        212.72000122070312,
        518.2536010742188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "1",
      "bbox": [
        213.91299438476562,
        513.9898681640625,
        218.14511108398438,
        521.0673217773438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ϵ",
      "bbox": [
        214.3179931640625,
        522.0477294921875,
        217.74513244628906,
        529.1251831054688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "e",
      "bbox": [
        225.947998046875,
        513.98974609375,
        229.87725830078125,
        521.0671997070312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "e−1",
      "bbox": [
        220.53799438476562,
        521.6013793945312,
        235.2851104736328,
        529.1251831054688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0000",
      "bbox": [
        238.30099487304688,
        514.5436401367188,
        243.29736328125,
        521.5254516601562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "log 1",
      "bbox": [
        243.30099487304688,
        513.98974609375,
        264.6371154785156,
        525.9334106445312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "δ + n(n + 1)/2 + n",
      "bbox": [
        260.4989929199219,
        516.24609375,
        350.3244934082031,
        529.6953735351562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0001m",
      "bbox": [
        350.3270263671875,
        511.2716369628906,
        361.0870361328125,
        521.5254516601562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "points taken from X0, U, and D, and",
      "bbox": [
        363.2900390625,
        516.24609375,
        522.0026245117188,
        526.9992065429688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "by PZ the probability measure corresponding to Z. Then the output (A, b) of Algorithm 1 satisﬁes",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        532.5150756835938,
        522.0044555664062,
        543.8795166015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the following probability inequality:",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        546.3367919921875,
        248.0073699951172,
        555.740478515625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PS(PZ( ˆR[t0,t1](A, b)) ≥1 −ϵ) ≥1 −δ;",
      "bbox": [
        216.14498901367188,
        566.5661010742188,
        395.8577575683594,
        581.3806762695312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(9)",
      "bbox": [
        509.280029296875,
        569.4976806640625,
        522.0000610351562,
        579.2504272460938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "or, in other words,",
      "bbox": [
        90.00003051757812,
        592.8547973632812,
        169.90916442871094,
        602.2584838867188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "PS( ˆR[t0,t1](A, b) is ϵ-accurate with respect to Z) ≥1 −δ.",
      "bbox": [
        178.3130340576172,
        613.0841064453125,
        433.6810607910156,
        627.899658203125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(10)",
      "bbox": [
        503.8260498046875,
        616.0166625976562,
        522.0006103515625,
        625.7694091796875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Proof If (A, b) is a feasible solution to (7), then ˆR[t0,t1](A, b) is by construction an ϵ-accurate",
      "bbox": [
        90.00006103515625,
        638.3360595703125,
        522.0009765625,
        653.1516723632812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "estimate. Algorithm 1 ﬁnds a solution to (8), which is the scenario problem corresponding to (7).",
      "bbox": [
        90.0001220703125,
        654.817626953125,
        522.0004272460938,
        664.5703735351562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The decision variables of (7) are a symmetric n × n real matrix and a real n-vector, so nθ =",
      "bbox": [
        90.0001220703125,
        667.5811767578125,
        522.00244140625,
        679.6574096679688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "n(n + 1)/2 + n. The N chosen in Algorithm 1 satisﬁes the sample bound in Theorem 1 for this nθ.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0001220703125,
        681.7420654296875,
        522.0004272460938,
        692.63623046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Therefore, the A and b that minimize (8) is a feasible solution to (7) with probability 1 −δ.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0001220703125,
        694.68017578125,
        488.0753173828125,
        705.2183837890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    }
  ],
